[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding JUnit Annotations Lifecycle: @Test, @BeforeEach, @AfterEach, @BeforeAll, and @AfterAll"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing unit tests in Java using JUnit 5, managing the test environment properly is crucial. JUnit provides lifecycle annotations that dictate when specific methods should execute relative to the test cases. Understanding this lifecycle ensures tests remain isolated, efficient, and maintainable."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Overview of JUnit 5 Lifecycle Annotations"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@BeforeAll: Runs once before all tests in the current test class. Must be static."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@BeforeEach: Runs before each individual @Test method in the class."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@Test: Marks a method as a test case to be executed by the test runner."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@AfterEach: Runs after each individual @Test method in the class."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@AfterAll: Runs once after all tests in the current test class have completed. Must be static."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Implementation Example"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example demonstrates how these annotations interact during a standard test execution cycle."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;

public class DatabaseConnectionTest {

    @BeforeAll
    static void setUpBeforeClass() {
        System.out.println("@BeforeAll -> Initializing database connection pool.");
    }

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        System.out.println("@BeforeEach -> Opening a new transaction.");
    }

    @Test
    void testDatabaseQuery() {
        System.out.println("Executing @Test -> testDatabaseQuery");
    }

    @Test
    void testDatabaseUpdate() {
        System.out.println("Executing @Test -> testDatabaseUpdate");
    }

    @AfterEach
    void tearDown() {
        System.out.println("@AfterEach -> Rolling back transaction and closing connection.");
    }

    @AfterAll
    static void tearDownAfterClass() {
        System.out.println("@AfterAll -> Closing database connection pool.");
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Execution Flow Analysis"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you run the DatabaseConnectionTest class containing two test methods, the console output will follow a strict, predictable sequence:"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. @BeforeAll executes once at the very beginning."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. @BeforeEach executes before the first test."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. First @Test method executes."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"4. @AfterEach executes after the first test."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"5. @BeforeEach executes before the second test."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"6. Second @Test method executes."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"7. @AfterEach executes after the second test."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"8. @AfterAll executes once at the very end."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always make @BeforeAll and @AfterAll methods static unless you change the test class lifecycle instance using @TestInstance(Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use @BeforeEach and @AfterEach to reset state or mock objects so that tests remain independent and avoid side effects."}]
